WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.925
The earth is round and your chart is flat, so every chart cheats a little.

2
00:00:05.175 --> 00:00:07.025
The question is how.

3
00:00:07.275 --> 00:00:11.000
Most nautical charts use the Mercator projection.

4
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:18.200
Its great gift is this: a straight line on the chart is a rhumb line, a line of one constant direction.

5
00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.775
You draw a line, read its direction, and steer it.

6
00:00:22.025 --> 00:00:24.400
That’s why navigators love it.

7
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.500
The price is stretch.

8
00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:32.550
To keep directions true, the latitude scale gets longer as you move away from the equator.

9
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:40.350
So on a chart that covers a lot of latitude, a mile measured near the top is drawn longer than a mile near the bottom.

10
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.450
What do you do about that?

11
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:48.825
Measure distance on the part of the latitude scale that’s level with where you’re working.

12
00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:52.275
Not at the top of the chart, and not at the bottom.

13
00:00:52.525 --> 00:00:54.500
Right beside your line.
